                    "message": "friendly ping for review, do we want to consider this patch or should i abandon it instead.."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the patch Alexander.\n\nWhen I run into these I often find myself wondering/needing to know what the unaliased type is.  I navigate to the alias and hit 'k' in my editor and the lsp server shows the unaliased type.  Not a big deal, but I guess for me that added step isn't really worth what we are getting here.\n\nI know this practice is common elsewhere in OpenBMC and I don't have a strong opinion on this.  Maybe we need some guidance in the c++ style guide?  @patrick@stwcx.xyz as the style guide owner, do you have any opinions here?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't have an opinion that should be of weight across the whole project.  \"Improved readability\" is in the eye of the beholder I guess.\n\nI generally use type aliases if I'm repeating myself or the type is a significant portion of the line."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> Not a big deal, but I guess for me that added step isn't really worth what we are getting here.\n\nSure, i can abandon the patch based on that reasoning. Thanks for the feedback \ud83d\udc4d"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/main.cpp",
                    "line": 303,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "we could probably make things much more succinct here another way:\n\n1 - why is a shared_ptr or a pointer at all even needed for a global variable?\n2 - we could make use of 'auto' here when declaring the variable type.\n3 - we could add 'using namespace std::chrono'"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/main.cpp",
                    "line": 303,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> 1 - why is a shared_ptr or a pointer at all even needed for a global variable?\n\nIt is being used for `shared_ptr` specific functions, for example:\n\n`globalStartTime != nullptr && globalStartTime.use_count() == 1`"
                }
            ],
